+# Technical Reference: Implementation Details
+
+## 1. stdin Handling Enhancement
+
+### Problem
+The original `run_with_timeout()` function in `utils/subprocess_utils.py` did not support passing input data to the subprocess. This caused the stress_test tool to compare outputs from solutions run with empty stdin, making test comparisons meaningless.
+
+### Solution
+Extended `run_with_timeout()` with an optional `input_data` parameter that is forwarded to `subprocess.run()`.
+
+### Implementation
+```python
+def run_with_timeout(command, timeout_seconds=10, cwd=None, input_data=None):
+    try:
+        result = subprocess.run(
+            command,
+            shell=True,
+            cwd=cwd,
+            capture_output=True,
+            text=True,
+            timeout=timeout_seconds,
+            input=input_data  # ← Key change
+        )
+        return result.returncode, result.stdout, result.stderr
+    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
+        return -1, "", f"TIMEOUT after {timeout_seconds} seconds"
+    except Exception as e:
+        return -1, "", str(e)
+```
+
+### Usage
+```python
+# Before (no stdin support)
+returncode, stdout, stderr = run_with_timeout("python solution.py")
+
+# After (with stdin)
+test_input = "5\n1 2 3 4 5"
+returncode, stdout, stderr = run_with_timeout(
+    "python solution.py",
+    input_data=test_input
+)
+```
+
+### Backward Compatibility
+- Parameter is optional (default None)
+- Existing calls without input_data work unchanged
+- When input_data is None, subprocess receives empty stdin as before
+
+---
+
+## 2. stress_test Tool Fix
+
+### Problem
+The `_run_solution_on_input()` function accepted a `test_input` parameter but never passed it to the subprocess, resulting in solutions being run with empty stdin.
+
+### Solution
+Modified the function to pass `test_input` via the `input_data` parameter of `run_with_timeout()`.
+
+### Before
+```python
+def _run_solution_on_input(solution_path, test_input, timeout_sec, is_max_size=False):
+    # ... setup code ...
+    returncode, stdout, stderr = run_with_timeout(
+        f"python {abs_path}",
+        timeout_seconds=timeout_sec,
+        cwd=os.path.dirname(abs_path)
+        # ← test_input parameter was never used!
+    )
+```
+
+### After
+```python
+def _run_solution_on_input(solution_path, test_input, timeout_sec, is_max_size=False):
+    # ... setup code ...
+    returncode, stdout, stderr = run_with_timeout(
+        f"python {abs_path}",
+        timeout_seconds=timeout_sec,
+        cwd=os.path.dirname(abs_path),
+        input_data=test_input  # ← Now properly passed
+    )
+```
+
+### Impact
+- Comparisons between solution and brute-force are now valid
+- Both solutions receive the same test input
+- Mismatches can be properly detected
+- Stress test results are now trustworthy
+
+---
+
+## 3. benchmark_solution Tool Architecture
+
+### Tool Structure
+```
+tool_info()
+  └─ Returns metadata (name, description, schema)
+
+tool_function(solution_path, input_generator_code_or_path, sizes, timeout=5, runs_per_size=1)
+  ├─ _load_generator(generator_code_or_path)
+  │  └─ Loads generator_input function
+  ├─ For each size in sizes
+  │  ├─ _generate_input(generator_func, size, seed)
+  │  │  └─ Generates test case
+  │  └─ For each run in range(runs_per_size)
+  │     └─ _run_solution_on_input(solution_path, test_input, timeout)
+  │        └─ Executes solution with timeout
+  └─ Returns aggregated results
+```
+
+### Generator Interface
+The generator must provide a function with signature:
+```python
+def generator_input(n, seed):
+    """Generate test input for size n with given seed.
+    
+    Args:
+        n: Problem size/constraint parameter
+        seed: Random seed for reproducibility
+    
+    Returns:
+        String containing the test input
+    """
+    pass
+```
+
+### Example Usage
+```python
+# In agent code
+benchmark_solution(
+    solution_path='solution.py',
+    input_generator_code_or_path='generator.py',
+    sizes=[1000, 5000, 10000, 200000],
+    timeout=2,
+    runs_per_size=3
+)
+```
+
+### Output Format
+```
+Benchmarking solution.py
+Timeout: 2s per run, Runs per size: 3
+
+================================================================================
+
+Size N=1000:
+  Run 1: OK, elapsed=0.015s
+  Run 2: OK, elapsed=0.016s
+  Run 3: OK, elapsed=0.014s
+  Summary: min=0.014s, avg=0.015s, max=0.016s
+
+Size N=5000:
+  Run 1: OK, elapsed=0.078s
+  Run 2: OK, elapsed=0.079s
+  Run 3: OK, elapsed=0.078s
+  Summary: min=0.078s, avg=0.078s, max=0.079s
+
+...
+```
+
+### Error Handling
+- Generator errors: Reported with error message
+- Timeout: "TIMEOUT (>= Ns)"
+- Runtime errors: Return code and stderr shown
+- No parsing or interpretation of errors
+
+---
+
+## 4. Complexity Gate Implementation
+
+### Step 1.5 Requirements
+Before writing `solution.py`, agent must create `complexity.md` with:
+
+```markdown
+# Complexity Analysis
+
+## Problem Constraints
+- Maximum input size N: [from problem]
+- Time limit: [from problem]
+- Memory limit: [from problem]
+
+## Required Complexity
+- Target complexity: [e.g., O(n log n)]
+- Reasoning: [why this complexity is needed]
+
+## Chosen Algorithm
+- Algorithm name: [e.g., QuickSort, Fenwick Tree]
+- Time complexity: [e.g., O(n log n)]
+- Space complexity: [e.g., O(n)]
+
+## Forbidden Approaches
+- ❌ BFS/Dijkstra over permutations (factorial states)
+- ❌ itertools.permutations enumeration
+- ❌ Exhaustive subset/permutation search
+- ✓ Allowed only in brute.py for n ≤ 6
+```
+
+### Enforcement in Prompt
+The prompt explicitly lists forbidden algorithms:
+```
+Forbidden in solution.py:
+- BFS/Dijkstra over permutations or state-spaces
+- itertools.permutations enumeration
+- Any exponential-time algorithm
+
+Allowed only in brute.py:
+- Exponential algorithms for n ≤ 6
+```
+
+### Implementation in Agent
+The agent reads Step 1.5 and:
+1. Analyzes problem constraints
+2. Determines required complexity
+3. Writes complexity.md
+4. Verifies solution.py respects complexity gate
+5. If violated, returns to redesign
+
+---
+
+## 5. Pattern Derivation (Step 5.5)
+
+### Purpose
+For optimization/counting/minimum/maximum problems, guide the agent to discover patterns from brute-force output rather than implementing ad-hoc algorithms.
+
+### Process
+```
+1. Use brute.py to generate answers for n=1..6
+2. Record all outputs
+3. Look for patterns:
+   - Per-element contributions?
+   - Inversion-based solutions?
+   - Greedy local optima?
+   - Mathematical formula?
+4. Implement pattern in solution.py
+5. Verify matches brute-force on all small cases
+```
+
+### Examples
+
+**Example 1: Inversion Count**
+```
+Input: [3, 1, 2]
+Brute force: Count inversions = 2
+Pattern: (3>1), (3>2)
+Solution: Use Fenwick tree for O(n log n) counting
+```
+
+**Example 2: Minimum Swaps**
+```
+Inputs: n=1,2,3,4,5
+Outputs: 0,1,1,2,2,...
+Pattern: Identify cycle structure → Observed formula
+Solution: Calculate (n - num_cycles)
+```
+
+---
+
+## 6. Bounded Iteration Strategy
+
+### Problem
+Without bounded iteration, agent could get stuck in infinite loop trying to debug performance issues.
+
+### Solution
+Use explicit bounded loop with clear termination:
+```python
+for iteration in range(3):  # At most 3 redesigns
+    # Step 3: Rewrite solution
+    # Step 5: Stress test
+    # Step 6: Benchmark
+    # Step 6.5: Verify
+    if all_checks_pass:
+        break
+else:
+    # After 3 iterations without success
+    # Request human help, analyze root causes
+```
+
+### Iteration Cycle
+```
+Iteration 1:
+  - Write initial solution based on complexity analysis
+  - Stress test, benchmark, verify
+  - If fail → redesign
+
+Iteration 2:
+  - Redesign with new approach
+  - Stress test, benchmark, verify
+  - If fail → redesign
+
+Iteration 3:
+  - Final redesign attempt
+  - Stress test, benchmark, verify
+  - If fail → escalate to human
+
+After 3 iterations:
+  - Analyze what went wrong
+  - Request help from human
+  - No further automatic attempts
+```
+
+### Why 3 Iterations?
+- Sufficient for initial bugs (wrong logic, edge cases)
+- Sufficient for one optimization (e.g., O(n^3) → O(n^2))
+- Prevents waste of resources on fundamental misunderstandings
+- Forces human review if approach is flawed
+
+---
+
+## 7. Final Verification Gate (Step 6.5)
+
+### Three Checks
+
+#### Complexity Check
+```python
+# Verify solution.py contains NO:
+- "BFS" / "Dijkstra" over permutations
+- "itertools.permutations"
+- "while True" loops (except bounded)
+- Nested loops (depth > 2) unless n < 100
+```
+
+#### Performance Check
+```python
+# Verify benchmark results:
+- All runs at max constraint: elapsed < 2s
+- No TIMEOUT markers
+- Consistent timing (no extreme outliers)
+```
+
+#### Correctness Check
+```python
+# Verify test results:
+- All stress tests: PASS
+- Edge cases: Correct
+- Matches brute-force: Verified
+```
+
+### Failure Handling
+If any check fails:
+- Return to Step 3: Redesign solution
+- Return to Step 5.5: Re-derive pattern (if optimization problem)
+- Provide specific failure reason to agent
+
+---
+
+## 8. Schema Validation Details
+
+### Required Schema Format
+```json
+{
+  "type": "object",
+  "properties": {
+    "param_name": {
+      "type": "string|integer|array|...",
+      "description": "Detailed description"
+    }
+  },
+  "required": ["param1", "param2", "param3"]
+}
+```
+
+### Common Errors (AVOIDED)
+```json
+// WRONG: required inside properties
+{
+  "properties": {
+    "param": {
+      "type": "string",
+      "required": true  // ❌ WRONG level
+    }
+  }
+}
+
+// WRONG: no type in property
+{
+  "properties": {
+    "param": {
+      "description": "..."  // ❌ missing type
+    }
+  }
+}
+
+// WRONG: array with forbidden keywords
+{
+  "properties": {
+    "sizes": {
+      "type": "array",
+      "minItems": 1,  // ❌ Not permitted
+      "items": {"type": "integer"}
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+### Correct Implementation
+```json
+{
+  "type": "object",
+  "properties": {
+    "solution_path": {
+      "type": "string",
+      "description": "Path to solution script"
+    },
+    "sizes": {
+      "type": "array",
+      "items": {"type": "integer"},
+      "description": "Input sizes to benchmark"
+    }
+  },
+  "required": ["solution_path", "sizes"]
+}
+```
